# Finding ancestors on demand
#
# Three parent links connect four people in a line: a, b, c, d. Two backward
# rules define an ancestor as a parent, or a parent of an ancestor. A forward
# rule asks who a's ancestors are and records each one it finds: b, c and d.
# The example shows a recursive backward rule answering a question with an
# open variable.

@prefix : <urn:example#> .

# The parent links.
:a :parent :b .
:b :parent :c .
:c :parent :d .

# Backward rule: a parent is an ancestor.
{
    ?x :ancestor ?y
} <= {
    ?x :parent ?y
} .

# Backward rule: a parent of an ancestor is an ancestor too. It calls itself
# one generation further on.
{
    ?x :ancestor ?z
} <= {
    ?x :parent ?y .
    ?y :ancestor ?z
} .

# Forward rule: everyone who is an ancestor of a is reached from a.
{
    :a :ancestor ?who
} => {
    :a :reaches ?who
} .
